pull_quote:
ROYAL SISTERS: Queen Elizabeth II and Princess Margaret.  By Anne Edwards. (Morrow, $22.95.) Elizabeth, the firstborn, was a serious child suited by temperament to the splendors and miseries of royalty; she liked dogs and horses. Margaret, four years younger, was a freer spirit; 

she 

liked Ethel Merman songs, jokes and nightclubbing, and was not exactly sure how she felt about the prospect of one day having to call her older sister "Your Majesty." They are the subjects of "Royal Sisters," a biographical look at a rather normal case of sibling rivalry, quite unexceptional -- except, of course, for its having been played out in Buckingham Palace. The girls advanced to the first and second positions in the line of succession to the English throne in 1936 when their Uncle David (Edward VIII) abdicated to marry the American Wallis Simpson. Thereafter during their adolescence, according to Anne Edwards, the future Queen grew increasingly conscious of the part history had marked out for her to play; Margaret, meanwhile, was occasionally confused and resentful over the greater amount of attention paid to her sister and the fact that her future position offered little scope for her talents other than trivial ceremony. ("I was born too late!" she complained.) This uncomfortable situation came to a climax in 1956 when, determined to avoid a repeat of the loss of royal prestige incurred in the abdication scandal, Elizabeth reluctantly but firmly refused Margaret permission to marry the divorced commoner Peter Townsend, a decision that may well have ended her hopes for personal happiness. There "Royal Sisters" ends; no information from the intervening 35 years is admitted. Ms. Edwards, whose previous biographical subjects include Queen Mary of England, Vivien Leigh and Judy Garland, is a meticulous, unsentimental biographer, and she works hard to breathe life into this melange of official history and gossipy anecdote, counterpointing it with sketches of significant personalities and events in the sisters' lives that are occasionally intriguing. It has to be said, though, that in the end her subject defeats her. For all their fascination, the English royals tend by design to lead lives of aggressive ordinariness beneath the carapace of monarchy; and here, as elsewhere, that ultimately precludes the generating of any real personal or narrative interest.
